{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices, especially from ~day 8 onward; apply a downward level shift that grows with horizon.",
    "Downtrend underreaction/lag: when the recent history is falling, the model projects stabilization/rebound too early; enforce stronger continuation of the recent negative slope for the first ~2–3 weeks.",
    "Rebound overshoot: after short-term upticks in history, the model extrapolates a larger/longer rebound than realized; dampen positive momentum signals and cap rebound magnitude.",
    "Horizon-dependent error: near-term (days 1–5) errors are small, but medium/long horizon (days 10–30) errors widen materially; increase mean-reversion strength downward (toward the prevailing lower regime) with horizon.",
    "Volatility/peak smoothing: model produces local peaks (mid-horizon) that are higher than realized; smooth forecast path by reducing amplitude of oscillations and lowering local maxima."
  ]
}
